1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Maybe 5, 10 percent.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Everyone else still says, oh, this guy's great.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Never passes back homework, rambles on, doesn't really expect anything.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Yeah, but he's great. Yeah, excellent.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
All right.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, a second motive that we see, which is also not in a standard model of selfish behavior is vindictiveness.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The desire for revenge.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
And we can see this very clearly in the ultimatum game.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:45,000
If you go and give significantly less than half in the ultimatum game, a lot of people will reject your offer, reducing their payoff and yours.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
And what possible motive could they have?

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Well, again, in an experiment, you can go and narrow it down so they don't know who you are, they don't know anything about you, they'll never find out.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:03,000
So why go and give up $4 to go and make the other player $6 poorer?

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Because you hate that person.

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Because you are angry.

15
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
I want to send them a message. What's the message?

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
I don't like you.

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
You made me mad. You hurt my feelings.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
And now I'm going to hurt your feelings.

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
You thought you could rip me off, but you can't.

20
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
So there's that.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
And this is true especially when we've been treated unfairly.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
So there's a connection between fairness and vindictiveness.

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
One of the most common reasons people have to act vindictively is that they think that the other side acted unfairly.

24
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
And so now I am willing to lose something in order to make you suffer.

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Although, it's unclear whether you want to call it vindictiveness, you might just call it spite.

26
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
So there are some experiments where you give someone a chance to go and hurt another person who hasn't done anything to you.

27
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
And a certain number of people will do that.

28
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:10,000
And it's like, here's the most successful, basically you give a test, everybody goes and gets paid based on the performance of the test.

29
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Your performance is based purely on your performance.

30
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
And then you go and ask people anonymously,

31
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
would you give up a dollar to take a dollar away from the person who got the high score on the test?

32
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
And a lot of people, maybe a third of people will say, yeah, hell yeah.

33
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
I'm going to wipe that smug look right off his face.

34
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
And you might say, why do you want to hurt this person?

35
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
This person didn't get the money by doing anything to you.

36
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Like, if you had done better, you would have gotten just as much as he had.

37
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
And it seems like, why should he have all the money and me not?

38
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Why, I ask you.

39
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
I'm going to go and show him, does he think he's better than me?

40
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Well, he won't after I ruin his life.

41
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
So there's that.

42
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
That is another reason.

43
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
It's just trying to hurt someone.

44
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
So there's the Ayn Rand phrase, hatred of the good for being good.

45
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
So I want to hurt those people. Why?

46
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Because they're successful and I'm not.

47
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:20,000
And you might even, in perspective, find that you too have felt this on some occasion.

48
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
You might say, well, it's because people believe the rule zero sum, but what if you make it totally clear?

49
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Like, it's not zero sum, it's positive sum.

50
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Now, I don't like the idea of anyone being more successful than me.

51
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Alright, now, notice that this is actually a psychological result where economists are being unduly optimistic about mankind.

52
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Because if everyone were purely selfish, how much energy would be put into spite?

53
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Anonymous spite. Non-repeat spite.

54
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
You might act like you want revenge if it's going to change someone's behavior,

55
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
but would you ever just lose money in order to hurt another person?

56
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
No.

57
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
But in the real world, people do this.

58
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:15,000
So anyway, there's this long-standing idea that economists have this ridiculously negative view of human nature.

59
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,000
But this is actually a way in which economists have an overly positive view of human nature.

60
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:29,000
So, again, which is not really a point in our favor, but it just means that the critics are missing a lot of what's going on in the world.

61
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Before pointing that out.

62
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:41,000
So, you just think about all the things that would never happen if economists were right about human nature.

63
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Like, how many murder-suicides would there be if economists were right about human nature?

64
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:52,000
What would spur a person to a murder-suicide?

65
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Like, you made me really angry.

66
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:02,000
So angry that I would rather be dead than live in a world where you're alive in it.

67
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
And that does not sound at all like what an economist thinks.

68
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Of course, murder-suicides are rare, unfortunately.

69
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
But they do happen.

70
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:19,000
So, you could really stretch your imagination and say, well, this is some kind of effort to go and send a warning to people.

71
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
But it's really quite a warning.

72
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
So, with inclusive fitness, there could be a reason to do it, right?

73
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
You might go and do this for the sake of your family.

74
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Like, someone goes and hurts your family, and maybe only if a month lives, you go and kill them and then kill yourself.

75
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
And then, are people going to mess with your family after that?

76
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
And, especially, suppose your family's reputation were doing this kind of thing.

77
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Like, we have a higher rate of murder-suicide in our family than any other family in the country.

78
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,000
So, you ought to be really nice to us.

79
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Don't break our hearts. Don't fire us.

80
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Don't do anything that would make us question whether we want to be alive or not.

81
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
So, you could go and tell that story, but it seems pretty far-fetched.

82
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:11,000
I mean, obviously, in a world where the economic model of narrow human selfishness were true,

83
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
there would be almost no suicide bombing or anything like that.

84
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
So, there's a lot of the worst things that we actually see in the world

85
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
that a model of pure human selfishness would say would not occur, actually.

86
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Here's another one.

87
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
How much pure bullying would happen in a world where everyone was selfish?

88
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
So, I mean, think about the bullies that you knew growing up. I assume none of you were the bully.

89
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Maybe, yeah, but even I bullied a couple of times.

90
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
You're like, who can you bully?

91
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
But, it did happen. But, anyway.

92
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
So, how much money was actually made by the bullies?

93
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,000
What financial gain? I mean, there's the stealing the lunch money bullying.

94
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
So, there is that kind. But, most bullying is more of, I'm just going to be mean to a person.

95
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Right? And, what is in it for that person?

96
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
The person who was doing the bullying, what did they get out of it?

97
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
But, mostly it's just like sadistic pleasure.

98
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:09,000
As well as the status among other people who also enjoy sadistic pleasures.

99
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
And, like in a world where everyone were perfectly selfish,

100
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
almost all this bullying would be wiped off the face of the earth.

101
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
And, people would at least treat each other with indifference.

102
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
It was the big improvement over what a lot of kids face, right?

103
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Just imagine if everyone who bullied you just acted like you were alive.

104
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
It would be a big gain.

105
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:36,000
Alright. So, alright. Now, real world significance of all this.

106
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Alright. So, easy to see this on some level.

107
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Alright. So, let's see.

108
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
So, like, you know, even when there's no repeated interaction,

109
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
we often give up small personal benefits to what fairness requires.

110
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Expect others to do the same.

111
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:55,000
Alright. And, you know, some of the more convincing cases,

112
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,000
so maybe prices and wages would move around more if these fairness norms are weaker.

113
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Especially wages. So, we'll get to that.

114
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
It's very unusual for nominal wages to ever fall.

115
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
Especially, like, their base nominal wage.

116
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
Bonuses are more flexible, but base nominal wages,

117
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and it seems that it's heavily driven based upon a widespread perception

118
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
that it's super unfair to ever cut someone's nominal wage by a penny or more,

119
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
by a penny per hour, and that only a monster would do this.

120
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,000
And, given that employers know that there's a very strong negative reaction to this,

121
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
they're afraid of the predictiveness that they would provoke in the workers,

122
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:33,000
and so we very rarely see noticeable changes in nominal pay.

123
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
At least nominal wages.

124
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Alright. And then there are some other markets where they're notoriously,

125
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:45,000
there are, like, prices are upwardly flexible, such as for concert tickets.

126
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:51,000
So, there are at least some cases where the concert tickets are clearly underpriced,

127
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,000
and then they get bought up really quickly, and then there's a rationing mechanism,

128
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
but scalpers figure out a way around the rationing mechanism,

129
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
and this is why you have to go and go through the security to show your monorobot

130
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
that it's just buying up tickets to resell them.

131
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:09,000
And again, why don't the original performers just go and raise the price?

132
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
So, there are some complex profit-maximizing stories saying things like,

133
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
well, they're concerned about not just their fans today, but the fans of the future,

134
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:22,000
and if you just charge the highest possible prices, then you are alienating young fans,

135
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
and the young fans are going to be around a lot longer,

136
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
and so it's all a very complex profit-maximization problem that you plan,

137
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,000
what is it you plan when you're 80 years old, and you're Simon and Garfunkel,

138
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
you want the people who are grateful to you for the cheap tickets they got 60 years ago

139
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
to come to your concert.

140
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Alright, so, there are some stories like that,

141
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:46,000
but the simple one that people usually say is they just think it's unfair to charge that much.

142
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:53,000
My brother once actually had Super Bowl tickets that he managed to get at the list price, like 200 bucks,

143
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and I think they got up to like $1,000, and then my brother did sell them.

144
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
Although, even there, he was wavering.

145
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:05,000
My brother's not an economist, so I would be the same, like, you know,

146
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:13,000
I wouldn't pay $1,000, so why would I give up $1,000 when I could sell them for that?

147
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Makes sense to me.

148
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
Alright, questions, questions, anyone happy?

149
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:28,000
Alright, so, now, another oddity,

150
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
this is one that's gotten quite a bit more attention in recent years,

151
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:35,000
almost entirely through papers where James Heckman is a co-author.

152
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:40,000
If you look at James Heckman's productivity, it is beyond all belief,

153
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
and you'll notice that they're almost all co-authors, so I think that explains a great deal,

154
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:49,000
but Heckman being famous and awesome in many ways can get co-authors that are really good,

155
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
and they'll probably let him put his name on it for 5% of the work, say.

156
00:10:52,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Also, a lot of his co-authors are former students, so it may be that they are repaying his pedagogy.

157
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
Possibly.

158
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Alright, so, there are a lot of economists who go beyond the assumption of constant preferences over time,

159
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
constant preferences means over time,

160
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:17,000
to the assumption of identical preferences for all humans.

161
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:23,000
What? All humans have the same preferences?

162
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:30,000
And yet, this is actually a view defended by two Nobel Prize winners, Stiglitz and Becker,

163
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
in a paper that was extremely influential.

164
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:39,000
And a lot of the argument came down to say, look, you can explain anything with preferences,

165
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:42,000
like why did the man drink motor oil? Because he liked the taste of motor oil.

166
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
Alright, that's pretty lame.

167
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:52,000
So, instead of giving us, ourselves, the infinite room to explain differences between people by differences between preferences,

168
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:01,000
why don't we go and look at different constraints that they face, or different income levels, and explain it that way.

169
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
The kind of most plausible examples they would have is,

170
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:12,000
suppose you see that people don't eat much ice cream during the winter, and they eat a lot of ice cream in the summer.

171
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:18,000
Does this mean that the preference for ice cream changes over the year?

172
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
And Becker and Stiglitz would want to say, no, there's a constant preference for ice cream,

173
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
but your desire for ice cream depends upon the weather.

174
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:30,000
So, the true fundamental preference is constant.

175
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:37,000
But, the appearance may be flexible because your circumstances have changed.

176
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:42,000
I mean, this would be really clear if it were, like, you want a snow shovel in the winter but not in the summer.

177
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Does this show that you like snow shovels in winter and don't like snow shovels in summer?

178
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:51,000
No, it shows that you have no use for the snow shovel in the summer, but you have a use for the snow shovel in the winter.

179
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,000
In both cases, your fundamental preference is for a drivable driveway.

180
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
And that's what's constant.

181
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:02,000
And then weather changes the constraints.

182
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:14,000
And then they start, well, alright, what would you say about librarians seeming to be people who like jobs that are quiet,

183
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
and like jobs where they talk?

184
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,000
They want to say, no, no, no, what's going on here is that each kind of worker invested in a different kind of human capital.

185
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,000
And now they invest in human capital, they are better.

186
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:32,000
The librarian is better at producing librarian services, and the salesman is better at producing salesman services.

187
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
But it's not because they have a fundamental difference in preference.

188
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
These are two different ways of making money.

189
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:48,000
And let's see, I think actually this is in a footnote, where there's this old story about why are there so many Jews in finance?

190
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:54,000
And then there's, you say, well, Jews just like finance.

191
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
So there's that one.

192
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:02,000
And then, but then there's a story going back a much longer time saying, no, in the past there were a bunch of regulations

193
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:07,000
which kept them out of all the other occupations, and finance was one of the few that was left.

194
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:13,000
So they went there, and once they're there, it's easy for them to pass those job skills on to their kids and their kids' kids.

195
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
And that's what's going on.

196
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
And Stigler and Becker did say, yeah, that sounds pretty good, although on a footnote they said,

197
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
that still doesn't explain why they just didn't convert to another religion.

198
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,000
And they said, well, we'll just leave that for future research.

199
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:34,000
But again, the spirit of it is to say, well, everyone would convert to whatever religion will actually give them the best deal.

200
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:40,000
And so, and again, this is where many people just start saying, really?

201
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
But anyway, can they really be saying what they appear to be saying?

202
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
And I'll say, yes, they are saying what they appear to be saying.

203
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:55,000
So whatever differences that appear to happen between people, it's really only on the surface that some people like one kind of music rather than another,

204
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
and that's just a raw, unexplained thing.

205
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:01,000
And instead, they say, no, no, what happened was that people like Chinese opera,

206
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:07,000
early on they were in a situation where there was a lower cost of building up Chinese opera appreciation capital.

207
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:13,000
And people who grew up liking rock and roll grew up in an environment where it was easy to acquire rock and roll appreciation capital,

208
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
and their fundamental preference is identical.

209
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:16,000
Yes?

210
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:24,000
So the librarian, if I can just use this example, like they have skills now, but we call those preferences,

211
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:28,000
how would you explain why they even decided to give those skills?

212
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
Because surely the librarian got the librarian skills because she likes it.

213
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:31,000
Right.

214
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:40,000
So the secret record story is, look, we are confident that if we were to go and do a very detailed study of the person's life story,

215
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:45,000
we would find that they had better opportunities for acquiring these librarian skills.

216
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:50,000
So any of that could just be that you grew up in a family of librarians or readers,

217
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,000
and other people grew up in a family of talkers, that kind of thing.

218
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
So you have better opportunities for acquiring certain skills,

219
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:01,000
and that explains the differences in what naive people have called preferences.

220
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:06,000
And again, so a lot of it comes down to is just a slogan.

221
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
It says, look, you can explain everything with preferences.

222
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:13,000
It's not falsifiable, and so we shouldn't go and appeal preferences to explain anything.

223
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:20,000
So I had a paper a while back, which was never cited by Heckman, but I'm not vindictive.

224
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:27,000
I'm just glad that someone prominent is pushing forward the ball of truth, even if I get no credit for it.

225
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:35,000
So anyway, what I point out in the paper is that there is a big literature in psychology on personality,

226
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:39,000
and there are many things you can say about it.

227
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:43,000
First of all, it seems like personality is a strong genetic component.

228
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
It's not just something that you learn through experience.

229
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:52,000
Secondly, personality is very predictive of a lot of outcomes,

230
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:58,000
and it's very nicely maps on to what we think about preferences.

231
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:02,000
So you find out what kind of personality librarians tend to have.

232
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
They tend to be introverts, salesmen extroverts,

233
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:10,000
and furthermore, it usually seems like they were this way from a very early age,

234
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
long before they were planning a career or anything else,

235
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:19,000
and furthermore, if you go and do Twitter adoption studies, you can see that this is not just appearance,

236
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
but actually there is a strong genetic component to personality.

237
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:26,000
So in this paper, I go over a lot of evidence from personality psychology

238
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:30,000
that indicates there is a lot of preference heterogeneity.

239
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:35,000
There are many people who just at a very gut level hate the stuff that you like,

240
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
and like the stuff that you hate,

241
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:43,000
and contrary to Stiglitz and Becker, there's no sign this can be reduced to their life experiences or anything like that.

242
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
Not that life experiences don't matter at all,

243
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:51,000
but that there's a lot of things about people that are there that seem to very deep set

244
00:17:51,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and have very little to do with any kind of a conscious or even subconscious decision to acquire a certain kind of capital.

245
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
So, for example, criminals.

246
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
Criminals have distinctive personalities.

247
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Like, they tend to be high in anger.

248
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
Wow.

249
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:14,000
And they tend to be low in empathy for others.

250
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,000
And they also tend to be lazy and impulsive.

251
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:24,000
And moreover, they were seen this way by adults in their lives from a very early age.

252
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:28,000
And is it so surprising that the people with these preferences

253
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:34,000
are more likely to pursue a career involving violence or robbery,

254
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:37,000
and of course end up in jail a lot?

255
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
Makes a lot of sense.

256
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
So, I mean, there's been quite a bit of work on the criminal personality,

257
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
which can be fixed very nicely in this, as well as on the genetics of criminality.

258
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:49,000
As many people get creeped out by the idea that there could be genes for crime,

259
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:50,000
but it really comes down to this.

260
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Do you think the genes could affect personality?

261
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
How could they not?

262
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
Do you think personality could affect crime?

263
00:18:58,000 --> 00:18:59,000
How could it not?

264
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
Well, then of course genes affect crime.

265
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:05,000
It's not that there's like the murder gene, but there's a bunch of genes

266
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
which tend to lead to a bunch of other things, which tend to lead to a bunch of other things,

267
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:13,000
and that's all we need to detect an effect of genes.

268
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
All right.

269
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
So questions about that?

270
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:21,000
So some of the best uses of personality psychology are for occupational choice.

271
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
It's not very predictive of income,

272
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
but it's very predictive of the kinds of jobs you're likely to have.

273
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:30,000
So you get a number of introverts in sales, very few.

274
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:35,000
A number of extroverts working as librarians, again, very few.

275
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
This was one that really struck me.

276
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:42,000
So in the Myers-Briggs personality test, there's a thinking versus feeling dimension,

277
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:46,000
and this is also the biggest gender gap,

278
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:50,000
where men come out much more thinking and women come out much more feeling.

279
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
But then if you go and look at males,

280
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:57,000
there is a male occupation that is extremely feeling,

281
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,000
where basically the men have a very feminine personality.


